{0 Performance}

We have compared the running time of OCaml programs executed natively,
interpreted by the bytecode interpreter, and executed by a JavaScript engine
after compilation by Js_of_ocaml or Wasm_of_ocaml.

With a state-of-the-art JavaScript engine (such as Google's V8), programs
usually run faster when compiled to JavaScript than with the OCaml bytecode
interpreter, and faster still when compiled to WebAssembly.

Benchmarks were run in October 2026 with OCaml 5.5 and Node.js 26.13 (V8 14.6),
on an OVH b3-16 instance (4 vCPUs, AMD EPYC Milan) running Ubuntu 26.04.
Unless stated otherwise, charts show values relative to Js_of_ocaml
(Js_of_ocaml = 1). Js_of_ocaml and Wasm_of_ocaml are run with [--opt=2]
(the default is [--opt=1]).

{1:execution-time Execution time}

{{image:files/performances/time.png}Relative execution times}

Code compiled by Wasm_of_ocaml runs more than twice as fast as code compiled by
Js_of_ocaml on average, and more than ten times faster on code that uses
exceptions heavily. It is slower on splay, which is dominated by string
operations.

{b Notes on Js_of_ocaml}:
- Exceptions are expensive (boyer, kb)
- String operations are slow (splay) because OCaml strings cannot be mapped directly to JavaScript's immutable UTF-16 strings

{1:code-size Code size}

{{image:files/performances/size.png}Relative size}

Most of the time, the generated JavaScript code is smaller than the bytecode file.
The benchmarks are small programs that do not make much use of any library.
The generated code remains smaller than the bytecode file for large programs
(ocamlc, js_of_ocaml).

The size gain can be much larger for programs that rely on external libraries,
thanks to dead code elimination.

The code generated by Wasm_of_ocaml, runtime included, is usually somewhat
larger than the code generated by Js_of_ocaml: about 20% on average, and
about 30% once compressed.
It consists of a JavaScript loader of about 16 to 20 KB, which accounts for
half of the output for the smallest programs, and a WebAssembly module.

{1:vs-handwritten Comparison with handwritten JavaScript}

{{image:files/performances/nativejs.png}Relative execution times}

Comparing execution times between handwritten JavaScript and generated code
shows that results are implementation-dependent. Code compiled by Js_of_ocaml
runs about as fast as the handwritten JavaScript on bdd, fft and fib. It is
faster on raytrace and slower on splay, which is dominated by string
operations. Code compiled by Wasm_of_ocaml is faster than the handwritten
JavaScript on bdd, fib and raytrace, as fast on fft, and slower on splay.

{1:optimization-levels Compiler optimization levels}

{{image:files/performances/size-optim-jsoo.png}Relative size}
{{image:files/performances/time-optim-jsoo.png}Relative execution times}

These charts compare the optimization level used in the other charts
([--opt=2]) with the default level ([--opt=1]), with [--opt=3], and with
some optimizations disabled. The
[-unsafe] variant is compiled from bytecode built with [ocamlc -unsafe].
- [--opt=1], [--opt=2] and [--opt=3] perform the same on these benchmarks.
- Dead code elimination matters most for size: without it, the generated code
  is 2 to 17 times larger.
- Without the optimization of calls, the code is about 20% larger and often
  slower (up to 1.7 times slower on bdd).
- Disabling inlining or compact output makes the code about 5% to 15% larger.
- Representing OCaml strings as JavaScript strings ([--disable=use-js-string]
  turns this off) has no measurable impact on speed on these benchmarks, and
  makes the code slightly smaller.
- Removing bounds checking ([-unsafe]) mostly helps array-intensive code
  (quicksort, soli).

{1:compile-time Compilation time}

{{image:files/performances/compiletime.png}Relative compilation times}

Js_of_ocaml and Wasm_of_ocaml compile the bytecode produced by [ocamlc]: their
bars include the time taken by [ocamlc] (the darker part), and the chart is
relative to [ocamlc] followed by Js_of_ocaml. The example programs
(boulderdash, …, ocamlc) are compiled from existing bytecode files, so no
[ocamlc] time is included for them. Compiling with [ocamlc] and then
Js_of_ocaml is about 10% faster than compiling with [ocamlopt] on these
programs.

At the default optimization level ([--opt=1]), Wasm_of_ocaml does not run
Binaryen, and compiles as fast as Js_of_ocaml (up to 30% faster on the
largest programs).

{1:effects-impact Effect handlers impact}

{2 Js_of_ocaml}

{{image:files/performances/size-effects-jsoo.png}Relative size}
{{image:files/performances/size-bzip2-effects-jsoo.png}Relative size (compressed with bzip2)}
{{image:files/performances/time-effects-jsoo.png}Relative execution times}

The performance impact of supporting effect handlers:

{b Size}:
- Code is about 5% larger with [--effects=cps], and about 15% larger with [--effects=double-translation], which duplicates the functions that may perform effects (up to 80% larger for ocamlc). Small programs where no function may perform effects are unchanged with [--effects=double-translation]
- The impact on compressed code is much lower since we add many function definitions, which are verbose in JavaScript but compress well
- Code using [Lwt], [Async], or [Incremental] can see a larger increase

{b Speed}:
- Almost no impact for small monomorphic programs
- Can be larger for code that heavily uses higher-order functions and polymorphism ([Lwt] code, for instance)

{2 Wasm_of_ocaml}

{{image:files/performances/size-effects-wasm.png}Relative size}
{{image:files/performances/time-effects-wasm.png}Relative execution times}

These charts are relative to the default Wasm_of_ocaml configuration.
By default, Wasm_of_ocaml implements effect handlers with JavaScript Promise
Integration ([--effects=jspi]), which does not require transforming the code.
With [--effects=cps], the code is slightly larger for small programs, about
10% larger for the examples, and up to 70% larger for ocamlc. Execution time
is mostly unchanged.
